WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.940
In an electronic world where a few taps on a
device keyboard sends an invitation or

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:09.770
greeting card, it's not all that common
anymore to receive something printed the old

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:10.820
fashioned way.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:15.890
Yet there's nothing quite as elegant as the
crisp recessed print that traditional

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:17.900
letterpress printing produces.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.830
Letter press printing is a centuries old
method that's making a comeback.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:28.870
The printing press makes an impression in
the paper with raised type as it applies the

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:34.880
ink. The printer first sketches out some
design ideas.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:41.180
Then he detours into digital technology,
finalizing the colors and composition and

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:43.790
choosing the font in traditional metal type.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.110
He cuts paper to the size of the greeting
card he's making.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:55.650
The paper is 100% cotton, which is more
pliable than standard wood fiber paper, thus

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.480
enabling a deeper impression.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:02.030
Cotton paper is more delicate, so he has to
cushion it while cutting.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.490
Otherwise the clamp holding the stack would
dent the paper.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.240
Now it's time to set up.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.480
He locates the selected font in the type
cabinet.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:19.440
Each individual character, called a sort, is
backwards because backwards type prints

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:26.360
forwards. He finds the swords for the first
line of text and lines them up left to right

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.670
and upside down in what's called a composing
stick.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:38.720
Once he completes the type of the line
called a form, he clamps it in position.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:45.850
Then he takes a metal frame called a chase
and mounts the form at a specific position

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:50.950
inside the frame, filling the excess space
with blocks of wood called furniture.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.780
Just below the piece of furniture that's
underneath the form.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:01.530
He places a compression device called a coin
and expands it with a key.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.200
This locks the form in position.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:10.740
He chooses each ink color from a fan deck,
then follows the recipe to mix it.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.750
This is color extender, which works like
paint thinner, diluting a dark color to a

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:19.800
lighter shade.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:27.330
He mixes the color extender into the ink,
blending the two ingredients thoroughly.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:36.140
When the color matches the fan deck sample,
he places a glob on a piece of cardboard and

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:37.910
takes the mixture over to the printing
press.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.510
He applies it in dots all over the ink disk.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:52.550
Then he pumps on the treadle to run the
rollers back and forth across the disc.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.270
This spreads the ink over the disc and onto
the rollers.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:01.940
He inserts the chase containing the form
into what's called the bed of the press.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:05.210
Now for the test run.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:11.120
The printer pulls the lever to bring the bed
and platen together, so that the form inked

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.420
by the rollers creates an ink filled
impression in the paper.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:22.230
If the print is off a bit, he marks the
correct position by pencil and installs gauge

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.330
pins which will hold the paper there.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.380
Now he's ready to start printing.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:31.270
There's no automation here.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.470
He manually places one sheet at a time in
the gauge pens.

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:41.770
The ink dries quickly because it's rubber
based and because cotton paper absorbs well.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:48.260
This printers technique is a hybrid of
traditional and modern approaches, as he uses

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:50.210
computer generated graphics.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:55.740
He sends the digital file to a company that
makes a polymer printing plate, which he

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:59.460
mounts on an aluminum base that fits in the
chase of the press.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:07.390
He switches plates to do the next graphic in
a different color layered over the first.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:19.710
The polymer plates marry the old and new
technology by making it possible to print

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:23.340
computer generated graphics by traditional
letter press.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:33.020
After printing the inside of this greeting
card as well, he uses a scoring rule mounted

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:37.040
in another press to make a perfectly
straight fold line down the middle of the

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:47.000
paper. The result is beautiful textured
print

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:51.980
on elegant cotton paper, just like the good
old days before.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.830
Inkjets e-cards and Evites.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:05:01.400
Bamboo ceramic lights add a touch of Zen to
any space made of ceramic and inspired by

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:07.520
bamboo. They resemble bundles of tropical
grasses, but they're not exact replications.

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:12.950
These light fixtures offer an artistic
interpretation of an exotic element from the

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:14.330
natural world.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:20.590
Trying to create a tropical ambience.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.310
It's all in the lighting.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:27.800
These ceramic lights celebrate the natural
beauty of bamboo and set the mood.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:35.210
Making the ceramic casing starts with a
slurry of clay and water known as slip.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:37.790
A propeller mixer evenly distributes the
particles.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.440
The slip flows through a hose and into
plaster molds.

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:47.690
The artisan controls the amount going in,
ensuring that each one is filled just below

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:53.350
the brim. Over three hours, the clay
thickens and congeals along the edges.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:58.400
After pouring out the remaining slip, he
trims the clay casting, which has a putty

00:05:58.400 --> 00:05:59.720
like consistency.

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:07.560
After leaving it to solidify a bit more, he
carefully disassembles the mold, revealing

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.470
the clay casting with a bamboo like detail.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:22.890
After curing overnight, the casting further
solidifies and the color lightens using a

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:28.140
knife. He adds more definition to some of
the detail and cleans up the rough edges.

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:34.560
He also shaves off protrusions formed where
the mold parts came together.

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:42.090
He lightly sands the entire bamboo ceramic
casting to smooth over any remaining

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:51.830
imperfections. Using a wet sea sponge, he
washes the entire

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:58.880
casting. This softens the clay on the
surface and gets rid of any minute scratches

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:00.200
left by the sanding.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:08.010
The artisan then cuts holes in the back of
the casting.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:12.810
One is for installing the light socket, and
the other is to run wires to the electrical

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:13.980
junction box.

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:22.410
With the clay surface still moist, he carves
nodes or joints into the bamboo canes.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:29.410
He also cuts wide vertical lines to create
the effect of cracks that occur in bamboo

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:31.270
naturally when they dry out.

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:38.200
After firing the clay casting in a kiln and
then cooling it overnight, the material has

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.990
become a strong, durable piece of ceramic.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:46.150
This craftsperson examines it for
imperfections and then begins the painting

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:52.210
process. She sprays a very light yellow
paint onto the bamboo ceramic.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:57.910
This gives the piece just a hint of color,
but it provides the desired undertone.

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:04.950
Next, she mixes a green oil paint with
thinner and then brushes the mix into the

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:06.360
grooves and notches.

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:08.760
This accentuates these areas.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:17.750
She also brushes it onto the edges of the
bamboo ceramic lightbox to better define the

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:23.890
profile. Using a soft cotton cloth.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:28.240
She blots up some paint from the palette and
wipes it on the ceramic.

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:33.730
This softens the brushstrokes and gives the
ceramic cane structure a hint of green.

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:40.630
She sets the light casing on a revolving
stand and inspects the coverage.

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:47.730
She then applies a liberal amount of brown
paint to the entire piece, and the brown

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:51.210
color completely overpowers the green and
yellow paints.

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:58.170
She rubs off a lot of the brown paint.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:02.520
This tones it down and subtly brings out the
other colors.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:07.620
This produces color variations that evoke
the different tones of dried bamboo.

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:10.980
The paint transforms the piece.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:16.560
A technician inserts the socket and pulls
the wires through one of the holes and out

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:26.560
the back. He secures the socket in the light
box from the back with a washer and nut, and

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:29.080
that completes this bamboo ceramic light
fixture.

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:36.330
Making. It takes roughly one week, but the
final result is very unique.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.140
While providing illumination.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:43.500
It may also be an object of conversation.

